<p><img src="https://cdn3.brettterpstra.com/uploads/2026/11/traverse-marked-header-rb.7542.jpg" width="800" height="226" style="margin:0 auto;clear:both;" class="header" /></p>
<p>I&rsquo;m in the middle of an end-of-year app roundup, and it is already past 7,000 words. A post that long, with this many moving parts, is a pain to manage as one Markdown file.</p>

<h3 id="headlines-are-not-the-problem">Headlines are not the problem</h3>

<p>MultiMarkdown Composer&rsquo;s table of contents is great when the structure is headlines. This list is not headlines. Every app is a term and one or more definitions. That is how I want it to read on the blog, and it is a bad way to reorder apps or jump to the one I am editing.</p>

<h3 id="one-app-in-markdown">One app, in Markdown</h3>

<p>A single entry looks like this:</p>

<div class="language-markdown highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight fixed"><code><span class="p">[</span><span class="nv">Hookmark</span><span class="p">](</span><span class="sx">https://hookproductivity.com/</span><span class="p">)</span> {% topapp 8 %} <span class="p">[</span><span class="nv">Hookmark on Setapp</span><span class="p">](</span><span class="sx">https://setapp.sjv.io/X4eM4o</span><span class="p">)</span>{:.setapplink}
: Once you get into the groove with it, Hookmark speeds up any project by keeping all of your related files at your fingertips, easy to jump between and reference. [...]
: Available on Setapp, or $29.99 regular, $69.99 pro.</code></pre></div></div>

<p>That is one app. Multiply it by a few dozen and the file turns into a wall.</p>

<h3 id="enter-traverse">Enter Traverse</h3>

<p><a href="https://apps.shakingthehabitual.com/traverse/">Traverse</a> is a new outliner. I have been working with the developer, Stuart Marshall, to make it fit the way I write in Marked. It already has <a href="https://shakingthehabitual.com/blog/traverse-using-marked/">built-in Marked support</a>, including Streaming Preview. I needed a little extra customization to get to my preferred format.</p>

<p><img src="https://cdn3.brettterpstra.com/uploads/2026/11/hookmark-metadata.jpg"></p>
<p>Traverse stores the outline as plain OPML, and every node can carry custom metadata. I used that for the URL, the price, a ribbon for how many years an app has made the list, the Setapp link and badge, and anything else that did not belong in the paragraph. Then I could look at the whole roundup as a horizontal outline, which is how my brain likes to work.</p>

<p>Paragraphs are Body nodes, not a notes field stuffed under the title. Stuart built an nvALT-like editor into the text and notes editors, so writing Markdown in there is actually pleasant. There is also a popout window when I want a larger font and a little more room.</p>


              <a class="zoom" href="https://cdn3.brettterpstra.com/uploads/2026/11/review-in-traverse-800@2x.7542.webp">
              <p><img src="https://cdn3.brettterpstra.com/uploads/2026/11/review-in-traverse-800.jpg"></p>
              </a>
              <figcaption>The review in Traverse</figcaption>
            

<h3 id="from-the-outline-to-the-post">From the outline to the post</h3>

<p>I wrote <a href="https://github.com/ttscoff/review-writer/blob/main/review-traverse">review-traverse</a> to read that OPML, metadata included, and turn it into the Markdown I publish. Liquid tags, Kramdown attribute lists, the whole mess. <em>As a side note, I learned a cool trick for writing a <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">.sh</code> script that runs as Ruby, which you can see in the script.</em></p>

<p>A second script in the same repo, <a href="https://github.com/ttscoff/review-writer/blob/main/review-ribbons">review-ribbons</a>, turns a tag like <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">{% topapp 1 %}</code> into the ribbon graphic.</p>


              <a class="zoom" href="https://cdn3.brettterpstra.com/uploads/2026/11/review-in-marked-800@2x.7542.webp">
              <p><img src="https://cdn3.brettterpstra.com/uploads/2026/11/review-in-marked-800.jpg"></p>
              </a>
              <figcaption>The review in Marked</figcaption>
            

<p>I run both as Marked Custom Rules actions, so the preview matches the blog. Marked&rsquo;s <em>Style Stealer</em> got me a copy of my blog&rsquo;s CSS, and a little Custom CSS in the <em>Style Generator</em> handles the Setapp badges. I open the OPML in Traverse and in Marked at the same time. The preview stays live, and scroll-to-edit jumps to whatever I am changing.</p>


              <a class="zoom" href="https://cdn3.brettterpstra.com/uploads/2026/11/custom-rules-in-marked-800@2x.7542.webp">
              <p><img src="https://cdn3.brettterpstra.com/uploads/2026/11/custom-rules-in-marked-800.jpg"></p>
              </a>
              <figcaption>Marked Custom Rules</figcaption>
            

<h3 id="publishing-and-going-back">Publishing, and going back</h3>

<p>When it is time to publish, I run <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">review-traverse opml2md</code> without <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">review-ribbons</code>. That writes the Markdown into my drafts folder with the original Liquid tags still in place. Jekyll plugins handle the final display.</p>

<p>If I tweak the Markdown instead, <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">review-traverse md2opml</code> merges those edits back into the OPML.</p>

<p>Side note, with all of these apps in structured data, I can also easily output a table, e.g. with linked app names and prices, if I want to summarize my work.</p>

<h3 id="a-template-not-a-recipe">A template, not a recipe</h3>

<p>Nobody else is going to want this exact setup. I am offering it as a template. The useful part is the combination: Traverse for the structure, Marked for the live view, and a small script between the outline and the post.</p>

<p>If you want to test it out though, grab the trials of <a href="https://apps.shakingthehabitual.com/traverse/">Traverse</a> and <a href="https://markedapp.com">Marked</a> (if you don&rsquo;t already have them). I can highly recommend this writing setup, even if you don&rsquo;t go the extra mile with the scripting.</p>

